---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/political-science/intermediate/political-ideologies">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Political ideologies are <strong>coherent packages of answers to the big political
    questions</strong> — who should decide, what equals fairness, what the state is for. Studying
    them properly means steel-manning each: understanding why millions found (and find) each
    answer compelling, not memorising attack lines.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the map: two axes, then the major families" widget="equationStep" config={ideoCfg} />

  <h2>How ideologies actually behave</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>They travel in bundles:</strong> positions on taxes, crime, immigration and family correlate — because they all flow from deeper commitments (equality vs liberty, universalism vs particularism). The bundle is the ideology.</li>
    <li><strong>They evolve:</strong> liberalism 1850 ≠ liberalism 2020; conservatism adapts to what it is conserving. Freeze-frame definitions always go stale.</li>
    <li><strong>Families, not monoliths:</strong> every ideology has internal wings — socialism spans communist to social-democrat; conservatism spans paternalist to libertarian. The fights within are often fiercer than the fights between.</li>
    <li><strong>Prediction from first principles:</strong> knowing a person's position on one axis moderately predicts the rest (the voting lesson shows how well) — ideology is a real cognitive structure, not a random list of opinions.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Burke, Reflections (1790); Mill, On Liberty (1859); Rawls (1971); Freeden, Ideology: A Very Short Introduction.</p>
</TopicPage>
